 filter unpicks what it hides (RealZeroP27 on X,
 `library-memory-select.test.cjs`).
 
+### Memory agents
+
+The work learn, organize and dream do is carried out by an agent the user
+picks, not a model: [internal/memagent/registry.go](../../internal/memagent/registry.go)
+holds `memory-agents.json` beside the `memory` folder, saying which memory
+agent each role runs as, on which magpie agent, model route and schedule.
+
+- An entry names a magpie agent (`internal/agent`): that agent's `AGENTS.md`
+  is the prompt, its `mcp.json` and skills the tools. `model` is an internal
+  route (`group/deepseek`), never offered in a dropdown.
+- `role` is one of learn, organize and dream, or several joined with `|`
+  (`learn|organize`): the design keeps one agent for the first two. An
+  agent doing both is listed under both.
+- A new registry starts from two presets (`Builtin`): `memory-curator`
+  (learn and organize) and `dreamer` (dream), both `Builtin: true`. A preset
+  may be removed or changed like any other; `Restore` brings back the ones
+  gone and leaves what the user changed as it is.
+- `defaults` names the agent a role runs as. `Registry.Default` follows it,
+  else the first agent carrying that role out; a default naming an agent
+  that isn't there, or one not carrying that role out, is repaired when a
+  change is made, and `Remove`/`Update` hand a role's default to another
+  agent that does carry it, or drop it. A default naming a role this magpie
+  doesn't know is left as it is, for a later magpie's role.
+- The file is read through `lastgood`: a missing one is the presets, and
+  one that can't be run (not JSON, no `agents` list, two entries with one
+  id, a bad role, a missing id, name or model route) is an error, never an
+  empty registry. It reads back from `<file>.bak` when that is good, and a
+  change refuses to write over a file it couldn't read. Before a write the
+  file there now is kept as the `.bak` (or copied aside as `.bad-<time>`
+  when it isn't valid); the new one goes through a temp file, flushed and
+  renamed over it (`steady.WriteFile`).
+- Every change takes `memory-agents.json.lock` (`filelock`, 10 s) and reads
+  the file afresh under it, so two magpies each keep their own change.
+  `TestConcurrentAddsAreAllKept`, `TestChangeRefusedWithoutTheLock`.
+- An empty `agents` list is a registry the user emptied, and reads: each
+  role then has no agent, and a run of it says so (`dispatch.go`).
+
+Routes, in [internal/gui/memory_agents.go](../../internal/gui/memory_agents.go):
+
+| Route | Does |
+| --- | --- |
+| `GET /api/memory/agents` | `{version, agents, defaults, roles, presets}`; `?role=` narrows to that role's agents |
+| `POST /api/memory/agents` | a new entry (`id`, `name`, `agent?`, `role`, `model`, `effort?`, `schedule?`, `scopes?`) |
+| `POST /api/memory/agents/update` | the entry with that `id`, replaced |
+| `POST /api/memory/agents/remove` | `{id}` |
+| `POST /api/memory/agents/default` | `{role, id}`: the agent that role runs as |
+| `POST /api/memory/agents/restore` | the presets a user removed, back as first made |
+
+Each change answers with the list as it is now; a refused one is a 400 that
+says why and writes nothing (`TestMemoryAgentRoutesCRUD`,
+`TestMemoryAgentRoutesRefuseUnreadable`). `scopes` is memory's own set
+(`global`, `project:<key>`, `project:*`, `agent:<id>`), trimmed, repeats
